//@version=5
indicator("QuantC ORB Long MES v2 - DRAFT, NOT LIVE", overlay=true, max_lines_count=50)

// ============================================================================
// DRAFT ONLY - saved separately from the currently LIVE orb_long_ghost.pine
// so nothing is overwritten. CORRECTION 2026-09-03: an earlier claim here
// said this file was "not wired to any TradingView alert or Ghost ticker" -
// that was wrong. A TradingView alert named "QuantC ORB Long MES v2 - DRAFT"
// and a matching Ghost ticker ("MES (v2 Draft)") already exist from a prior
// version of this file, predating this session's C1/C2/M1-M4 fixes. Verified
// via Ghost's Diagnose panel: that alert last fired 2026-08-28 and shows
// "Stopped manually" in TradingView's Alerts panel, same day everything else
// was paused - it is dormant, not a live risk, and (testMode defaulting to
// true) would only ever have sent test:true dry-run alerts while it ran. But
// it is NOT nothing, and the code here has changed significantly since it
// was last live. Before reusing that alert/ticker: delete or replace the old
// TradingView alert's message with this file's current alert() payload, and
// confirm test mode / hard gates are what you intend before turning it back
// on.
//
// What changed vs the live script, and why (each change traces to a
// measured research result - see mes_final_run.py / dynamic_sizing_matrix.py
// in this folder):
//
//   1. Entry window LOCKED at the full 09:45-16:00 ET session - this is
//      config 4 from mes_final_run.py, measured at 77.8% pass (PRE-M1
//      figure - see item 3/5 below; this specific full-session-vs-narrow-
//      window comparison has not been re-run under the post-M1 mechanism).
//      A narrower 09:45-11:00 window was also tested (config 5) and
//      REJECTED: it only drops 1.7% of historical trades but cost ~6
//      points of survival odds (72.0% vs 77.8%), a larger effect than the
//      trade count alone would suggest. Full session was the stronger,
//      measured choice at the time; re-test before assuming the same
//      ranking still holds post-M1.
//   2. A broker-side stop-loss is sent with the entry alert (`sl` field).
//      Stop distance is DERIVED from a per-contract dollar-risk cap
//      (`riskPerContract`), not a fixed point constant: stop =
//      riskPerContract / syminfo.pointvalue. This mechanism was added 2026-09-03
//      after the original fixed 124.75-point 99th-percentile-MAE stop
//      (wide_stop_test.py, $623.77 on 1 MES) was flagged as an oversized
//      single-trade risk relative to this account's $1,800 daily limit.
//      FIRST TRIED $270 (landing just under MES's measured 90th-percentile
//      MAE), then ACTUALLY BACKTESTED it - see mes_c1_stop_rerun.py, full
//      5-year history, same cost model and firm rules as mes_final_run.py.
//      Result (PRE-M1 figures - both sides of this comparison used the
//      time-exit's original one-time checkpoint, item 3/5 below; the
//      relative conclusion below has not been re-verified post-M1): the
//      $270/54-point stop fires 5.7% of the time once combined with the
//      90-min time-exit (vs the old stop's 0.2%), and that extra firing
//      measurably HURT survivability - 74.60/67.58/59.51% pass (no/1-tick/
//      2-tick slippage) vs the old stop's 77.82/71.60/64.29%. Reason: this
//      strategy trades at most once per day, so a
//      single trade's stop-out was never actually close to breaching the
//      $1,800 DAILY limit at either distance - the metric that actually
//      determines eval survival is the $3,000 TOTAL trailing drawdown,
//      and on that metric a stop wide enough to rarely fire (letting the
//      time-exit do the real risk-cutting) beats a tighter one that fires
//      often enough to double up with it. riskPerContract is now $625
//      (125.0 pts, ~ the original 124.75pt distance) - the daily-limit-
//      percentage framing that motivated $270 was not the metric that
//      predicted outcomes; measured 5-year survivability was. The dollar-
//      cap MECHANISM is kept (still tied to account size, still a labeled
//      input instead of a magic constant) even though the chosen distance
//      now lands back near where research already said it should be.
//      SCOPE CHANGE 2026-09-10: the cap is now per CONTRACT, not per trade.
//      It was previously stop = maxRiskDollars / (qty x pointvalue), which
//      silently HALVED the stop distance the moment qty rose above 1 - i.e.
//      raising size would have recreated the exact too-tight-stop failure
//      measured live on MNQ (5 of 5 trades stopped out inside 2 minutes on a
//      10pt bracket no backtest ever approved). Per-contract makes stop
//      DISTANCE invariant to qty, so contract count now scales dollar risk
//      and expectancy together without disturbing the validated 125pt stop.
//      VERIFIED against QuantCrawler's own Ghost Webhook JSON Reference
//      (https://quantcrawler.com/guides/ghost-webhook-json, checked
//      2026-08-21): `sl` is documented as an "Absolute stop price" (a
//      price level, not a point/tick offset) - confirming the value this
//      script sends (close - stopPoints, a price) is the correct shape.
//      That page also states the stop needs BOTH "Use Stop Loss and Take
//      Profit" AND "Use Webhook SL/TP" turned on for the ticker - TWO
//      toggles, not the one SETUP_GUIDE.md currently mentions - and that
//      "a stop on the wrong side of entry is discarded" (not a concern
//      here - this stop is always below a long entry).
//   3. A 90-minute "underwater" time exit: from 90 minutes after entry
//      onward, if the position is not net profitable (after an estimated
//      cost), send a market exit alert immediately rather than holding to
//      the close. UPDATED 2026-09-03 (QC report M1): re-checked on EVERY
//      bar past the 90-minute mark, not just the first one. The original
//      one-time checkpoint (matching mes_final_run.py's original
//      resolve_exit()) meant being green by a single tick at exactly that
//      one bar disabled the underwater exit for the rest of the day, even
//      if the trade went underwater later. Backtested both ways on the
//      full 5-year history before changing (mes_m1_m4_checks.py): re-
//      checking every bar raised the pass rate from 77.80% to 84.27% and
//      even eliminated the rare stop-loss hits entirely (they're now
//      almost always caught by the time-exit first) - a real, broad
//      improvement across the sample, not a single-trade fluke. NOTE this
//      changes exit timing broadly enough that the H3 take-profit sweep
//      (mes_takeprofit_sweep.py, which tested against the pre-M1 time-exit)
//      and the entry-window/C1 comparisons in items 1-2 above have not been
//      re-verified under this mechanism - directionally likely to still
//      hold, not re-confirmed. The exit alert's "reason" field is custom
//      (not one of Ghost's
//      documented exit_type values, which are tp/sl/trail/be - none fit a
//      time-based exit) - safe to send anyway, since Ghost's docs say
//      unknown fields are ignored, not rejected.
//   4. A hard gate if this is not running on MES / the 1-minute chart
//      (MNQ was tested and closed - 30-35% pass rate, discarded per your
//      instruction). `validSymbol` and `validTimeframe` are ANDed directly
//      into `longSignal`, so no entry alert can fire on the wrong chart -
//      this is enforced in-script, not just a label. TradingView alerts
//      still can't be restricted to one ticker from the outside, so you
//      still need to create the TradingView alert on the MES1! chart only
//      and not duplicate it onto MNQ1!, same as any other Pine alert - but
//      if that ever happens by mistake, this script will now refuse to
//      trade rather than firing with a mis-scaled stop.
//
//   5. Execution slippage, applying 1-2 adverse ticks to EVERY exit fill -
//      the stop, the 90-min time exit, AND the scheduled session close.
//      RE-MEASURED 2026-09-03 after the M1 fix (item 3) changed the time-
//      exit mechanism - the original 77.82/71.60/64.29% figures (measured
//      2026-08-21, before M1) are superseded, not just re-labeled:
//        no slippage ................................... 84.27% pass
//        1 tick  (0.25 pt) adverse, all exits .......... 77.64% pass
//        2 ticks (0.50 pt) adverse, all exits .......... 68.78% pass
//      Every row improved over its pre-M1 counterpart (roughly +6-7 points
//      at every slippage level) - the fix is not a one-off, it holds up
//      once realistic fill quality is applied too. Take the 84.3% headline
//      as optimistic - realistic live fills land somewhere between the
//      1-tick and 2-tick rows above, not at the top.
//
// STILL OPEN, not yet resolved before this should go live:
//   - In Ghost's dashboard, the MES ticker needs BOTH "Use Stop Loss and
//     Take Profit" AND "Use Webhook SL/TP" turned ON for the `sl` field to
//     do anything - SETUP_GUIDE.md's current instructions say to leave the
//     first one OFF (that guide describes the OLD, no-stop mechanism).
//     Both toggles are manual changes in Ghost's UI, not something this
//     script can do - yours to flip deliberately, same as Test mode.
//
// RESOLVED, no code change needed:
//   - QC report M4 (unfiltered gap/extension entries): tested requiring the
//     breakout close to stay within 1.0/1.5/2.0x the opening range's own
//     width beyond rangeHigh, on the full 5-year history
//     (mes_m1_m4_checks.py). Zero of 662 historical trades exceeded even
//     the tightest (1.0x) cap - because entry fires on the FIRST 1-min bar
//     that closes above rangeHigh, extensions beyond the range are already
//     naturally small by construction. The concern doesn't materialize for
//     this entry rule; no filter added.
//   - QC report H4 (long-only design): this script only enters on breakouts
//     above rangeHigh - breakdowns below rangeLow are tracked (for the
//     range itself) but never traded. Discussed 2026-09-03: a short-side
//     mirror is a genuinely different, unvalidated strategy (downside
//     moves in index futures often behave asymmetrically - faster, more
//     gap-prone), not a free extension of this one, and adding a second
//     unvalidated strategy to a live eval account carries real risk.
//     Decision: leave this long-only by design. If revisited, a short
//     mirror should get the same treatment as every other change here -
//     backtested standalone against the full 5-year history before ever
//     touching Ghost - not bolted on directly.
// ============================================================================
//
// QC REPORT STATUS: C1/C2/C3 (critical), H1/H2/H3/H4 (high), M1/M2/M3/M4
// (medium) are all closed as of 2026-09-03 - see items above for what
// changed, and this note for what didn't.

qty            = input.int(1, "Contracts per signal", minval=1, maxval=1, tooltip="LOCKED at 1 by decision 2026-09-11. Stop DISTANCE is unaffected by this value (see 'Max $ risk per CONTRACT'). Measured 2026-09-10 on the full 5-year history at 1 tick slippage, starting from the account's real state: 1/2/3/4 contracts = 77/52/43/39% chance of passing, with median time-if-passing ~3.7yr / 1.2yr / 7mo / 5mo. More size does NOT improve the odds - it buys speed by selling certainty. Hard-locked at 1 so a live chart cannot be nudged; raising it needs a deliberate edit to maxval in source. Per-trade dynamic ATR sizing is a separate idea that was tested and rejected.")
testMode       = input.bool(true, "Test mode (Ghost dry-run, no real orders)", tooltip="Adds \"test\":true to every alert. Turn OFF only when you are ready for signals to place real orders - that switch is yours to make deliberately.")
riskPerContract = input.float(625, "Max $ risk per CONTRACT (stop is derived from this)", minval=1, tooltip="Per CONTRACT, not per trade: stop distance = this / point value, so changing 'Contracts per signal' scales total risk WITHOUT moving the stop. Total risk per trade = this x contracts. Default ($625, ~125 pts) was chosen after backtesting a tighter $270 cap on the full 5-year history and finding it measurably hurt survivability (mes_c1_stop_rerun.py) - this value lands back near the original 99th-percentile-MAE distance (124.75 pts) that research already validated. See the header comment for the full story.")
timeExitMin    = input.int(90, "Underwater time-exit, minutes after entry", minval=1)

enableFundedPhaseCap  = input.bool(false, "Funded phase: cap profit per trade (Lucid 40% consistency rule)", tooltip="OFF during evaluation - the eval has no consistency rule, so capping profit here would only give away progress toward the $6,000 target for no benefit (see the header comment). Turn ON once funded, where a single oversized day can breach the rule that no day may exceed 40% of the payout cycle's total profit.")
maxDailyProfitDollars = input.float(300, "Max profit per trade before force-exit (funded phase only)", minval=1, tooltip="Only used when the toggle above is ON. Default $300 = 40% of the confirmed $750/cycle funded payout target - edit if your funded terms differ. Since this strategy trades at most once per day, capping per-trade profit is equivalent to capping daily profit.")

stopPoints = riskPerContract / syminfo.pointvalue

validSymbol    = syminfo.ticker == "MES1!" or syminfo.root == "MES"
validTimeframe = timeframe.period == "1"

if barstate.islast and not validSymbol
    label.new(bar_index, high, "BLOCKED: this draft is validated for MES only.\nCurrent chart: " + syminfo.ticker + "\nMNQ was tested and rejected - no signal will fire here.", style=label.style_label_down, color=color.red, textcolor=color.white)

var float rangeHigh       = na
var float rangeLow        = na
var int   rangeBarCount   = 0
var bool  tradedToday     = false
var bool  inPosition      = false
var float entryPrice      = na
var int   entryTime       = na

rangeWin        = not na(time(timeframe.period, "0930-0945", "America/New_York"))
fullSession     = not na(time(timeframe.period, "0945-1600", "America/New_York"))
sessionLastBar  = not na(time(timeframe.period, "1559-1600", "America/New_York"))
rangeWinStart   = rangeWin and not rangeWin[1]

if rangeWinStart
    rangeHigh       := high
    rangeLow        := low
    rangeBarCount   := 1
    tradedToday     := false
else if rangeWin
    rangeHigh       := math.max(rangeHigh, high)
    rangeLow        := math.min(rangeLow, low)
    rangeBarCount   += 1

rangeComplete = rangeBarCount >= 15
longSignal    = fullSession and not tradedToday and rangeComplete and close > rangeHigh and validSymbol and validTimeframe

minutesSinceEntry = inPosition ? (time - entryTime) / 60000 : na
pastTimeExitCutoff = inPosition and fullSession and minutesSinceEntry >= timeExitMin
timeExitTriggered   = pastTimeExitCutoff and close <= entryPrice   // "underwater or breakeven" per the spec, re-checked every bar past the cutoff (M1 fix - was a single one-time checkpoint, backtested and confirmed to measurably help: 84.27% vs 77.80% pass, mes_m1_m4_checks.py)
unrealizedProfitDollars = inPosition ? (close - entryPrice) * qty * syminfo.pointvalue : na
profitCapTriggered  = enableFundedPhaseCap and inPosition and unrealizedProfitDollars >= maxDailyProfitDollars
closeExitSignal     = sessionLastBar and inPosition
catchAllFlatten     = inPosition and not fullSession   // half-day / early-close backstop: sessionLastBar's fixed 1559-1600 window never arrives on a day that closes early, so flatten the moment the session window itself ends instead of waiting for that fixed clock time
exitSignal           = timeExitTriggered or profitCapTriggered or closeExitSignal or catchAllFlatten

tsIso() =>
    str.format_time(time_close, "yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss'Z'", "UTC")

if longSignal
    tradedToday     := true
    inPosition      := true
    entryPrice      := close
    entryTime       := time
    stopPrice = close - stopPoints
    msg = '{"action":"buy","price":' + str.tostring(close) + ',"qty":' + str.tostring(qty) +
          ',"sl":' + str.tostring(stopPrice) +
          ',"time":"' + tsIso() + '"' + (testMode ? ',"test":true' : '') + '}'
    alert(msg, alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

if exitSignal
    inPosition := false
    reason = profitCapTriggered ? "funded_profit_cap" : timeExitTriggered ? "time_exit_90min" : catchAllFlatten ? "catch_all_flatten" : "session_close"
    msg = '{"action":"exit","reason":"' + reason + '","time":"' + tsIso() + '"' + (testMode ? ',"test":true' : '') + '}'
    alert(msg, alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

plot(rangeComplete and fullSession ? rangeHigh : na, title="Opening range high", color=color.new(color.green, 0), style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=1)
plot(rangeComplete and fullSession ? rangeLow  : na, title="Opening range low",  color=color.new(color.red, 0),   style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=1)
plot(inPosition ? entryPrice - stopPoints : na, title="Stop-loss level", color=color.new(color.orange, 0), style=plot.style_circles, linewidth=1)
plotshape(longSignal, title="Long signal", style=shape.triangleup, location=location.belowbar, color=color.green, size=size.small)
plotshape(exitSignal, title="Exit signal", style=shape.xcross, location=location.abovebar, color=color.gray, size=size.tiny)

if barstate.islast and not validTimeframe
    label.new(bar_index, high, "BLOCKED: this script is only validated on the 1-minute chart.\nCurrently running on: " + timeframe.period + "\nNo signal will fire here.", style=label.style_label_down, color=color.red, textcolor=color.white)
